# lspbridge.service — systemd USER unit template for the lspbroker daemon
# (github.com/davidwalter0/lspbridge/cmd/lspbroker).
#
# Installed by `make install-service`, which runs a whole-file `sed` over
# this template to fill in the placeholders below (binary / socket path /
# spawn PATH — see the Makefile's LSPBROKER_EXEC / LSPBROKER_SOCKET /
# LSPBROKER_PATH variables) and writes the result to
# ~/.config/systemd/user/lspbridge.service (or an override dir when
# staging). NOTE: because the substitution is whole-file, this comment
# block deliberately avoids spelling out the placeholder tokens themselves
# — doing so here would let sed mangle this prose too. Look at the
# [Service] section below to see the tokens in place.
#
# This is a USER unit (not system-scope) by design: no root, no sudo. The
# daemon spawns per-project language servers (pyright, typescript-language-
# server, rust-analyzer, the Dart Analysis Server, ngserver) on behalf of
# whichever user is running an editor/agent session, and has no business
# running as anyone else. See docs/design/broker-lifecycle-and-socket-path.org
# for the full deployment story.
#
# WHY THE SOCKET PATH IS PASSED EXPLICITLY, NOT LEFT TO THE DEFAULT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# lspbroker's built-in default is $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lspbridge/broker.sock when
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is set, else <TMPDIR-or-/tmp>/lspbridge-<uid>/
# (pkg/broker.DefaultSocketPath, which falls through to os.TempDir() —
# itself $TMPDIR-or-"/tmp" — in the empty-XDG_RUNTIME_DIR branch). A
# systemd --user unit ALWAYS has XDG_RUNTIME_DIR set (systemd exports it to
# every unit it spawns) — so left to its default, this daemon would bind
# under /run/user/<uid>/. But the daemon's real consumers (mcp-ast,
# mcp-agent-editor) are spawned by the mgk MCP gateway under a FILTERED
# environment with NEITHER XDG_RUNTIME_DIR NOR TMPDIR set (verified live
# 2026-08-12 against /proc/<mcp-ast-pid>/environ), so they fall through both
# branches and land on the literal "/tmp" default — a path this daemon was
# never listening on if left to ITS OWN default. That is a silent mismatch,
# not a crash: every semantic query just fails as "broker not running" even
# though it is — see the design doc above for the full trace. So this unit
# pins the socket to the path the CONSUMER resolves, closing the gap
# explicitly instead of relying on two processes agreeing on a default they
# do not share. Do not "simplify" this back to the bare default, and do not
# reintroduce a $TMPDIR-aware formula on the Makefile side either — the
# INSTALLING shell's TMPDIR is a different, irrelevant environment (see the
# Makefile's LSPBROKER_SOCKET comment for the specific bug that produced).
#
# WHY A PLAIN ALWAYS-ON SERVICE, NOT SOCKET ACTIVATION
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# lspbroker has no systemd socket-activation support today — nothing in
# cmd/lspbroker reads LISTEN_FDS or accepts an inherited listener. An
# Accept=no [Socket] unit would hand it descriptors it simply ignores, and
# it would fall back to binding the path itself anyway. Separately, the
# daemon's own -idle-timeout reaps idle language-server SESSIONS, not the
# broker process itself — the broker is meant to be long-lived by design.
# Socket activation is a real option for later (tracked in the design doc)
# but needs an ExecStart-adjacent change to cmd/lspbroker/main.go first;
# this unit does not assume it.
#
# WHY THE SPAWN PATH IS SET EXPLICITLY
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# This daemon's entire job is to exec.LookPath and spawn language servers.
# A systemd --user unit's default PATH is a minimal system one (no
# ~/.local/bin, no ~/.cargo/bin — measured 2026-08-10), so left unset the
# unit would start cleanly, report active, and then fail every single
# query with a missing-binary error: healthy-looking and non-functional at
# once. The value below is captured from the shell that ran
# `make install-service`, not invented — re-run install-service from a
# shell that can already find these tools if you add or move one.
[Unit]
Description=lspbroker — shared LSP session broker (lspbridge)
After=network.target

[Service]
Environment=PATH=__PATH__
ExecStart=__EXEC__ -socket __SOCKET__
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=3

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
